  "worked_solution": "An expression is a tautology if it is always true, regardless of the inputs.\n\nBy completing the final column in the truth table below, we obtain the following:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧Y\\,$ | $\\,(¬X⇒Y)⇒X\\,$ | $\\,(¬X∧Y)∨[(¬X⇒Y)⇒X]\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $(¬X∧Y)∨[(¬X⇒Y)⇒X]$ \n$\\text{is}$\nalways true. Therefore, it \n$\\text{is}$\na tautology.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
